## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinishTimes(n, edges, durations) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  const finish = durations.slice();
  let seen = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    seen++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + durations[v]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (seen !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return finish;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple start times or optional prerequisites?
- How does the solution change if edge weights represent communication delay instead of task duration?

## Why This Is Asked
In practice, data often contains many duplicates. A 3-way partitioned quicksort minimizes comparisons on duplicates and scales with the number of distinct keys.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- Randomized pivot to avoid worst-case
- Complexity: O(N log D) expected when D distinct keys
- In-place sort with minimal extra space

## Code Example
```javascript
function sort3(a, l=0, r=a.length-1){
  if (l >= r) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random()*(r-l+1))+l];
  let i=l, lt=l, gt=r;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  sort3(a, l, lt-1);
  sort3(a, gt+1, r);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for stable sorting with duplicates?
- What are the trade-offs vs a counting/radix approach on integers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the integration of fundamental data structures—arrays, linked lists, hashmaps, and heaps—to solve a streaming scenario with sliding window deduplication and real-time ranking.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window deduplication per (user_id, action) using a hashmap with timestamp tracking
- Per-user score aggregation maintained in a hashmap for O(1) updates
- Global top-K ranking via max-heap with O(log K) insertion/deletion and lazy invalidation
- Efficient expiration handling using linked lists and amortized cleanup strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
class EventProcessor {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.latestByUserAction = new Map(); // (user_id,action) -> timestamp
    this.userTotals = new Map(); // user_id -> total_score
    this.userEvents = new Map(); // user_id -> linked list of (timestamp, score)
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap(); // (total_score, user_id)
  }

  addEvent(event) {
    const key = `${event.user_id}:${event.action}`;
    const latest = this.latestByUserAction.get(key);

    if (!latest || event.ts - latest > this.W) {
      // Update dedup map
      this.latestByUserAction.set(key, event.ts);

      // Update user total
      const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(event.user_id) || 0;
      const newTotal = currentTotal + event.score;
      this.userTotals.set(event.user_id, newTotal);

      // Add to user's event list
      if (!this.userEvents.has(event.user_id)) {
        this.userEvents.set(event.user_id, new LinkedList());
      }
      this.userEvents.get(event.user_id).push({ts: event.ts, score: event.score});

      // Update heap
      this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: event.user_id});
    }
  }

  getTopK(k, now) {
    this.expire(now);
    return this.maxHeap.getTopK(k);
  }

  expire(now) {
    for (const [userId, events] of this.userEvents) {
      while (events.length > 0 && now - events.head.ts > this.W) {
        const expired = events.shift();
        const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId);
        const newTotal = currentTotal - expired.score;
        this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);

        if (newTotal > 0) {
          this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: userId});
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
**Time:** O(1) amortized per event for updates, O(log K) for top-K retrieval
**Space:** O(N) where N is number of active users plus events within window W

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Design a structure to maintain the k-th largest element efficiently in a streaming context, using a heap and a fixed-size container.

## Key Concepts
- Min-heap for thresholding top-k
- Time: O(log k) per insert
- Edge cases: fewer than k elements, duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class KthLargest {
  constructor(k, nums = []) {
    this.k = k; this.heap = [];
    for (const n of nums) this.add(n);
  }
  add(val) {
    if (this.heap.length < this.k) { this.heap.push(val); this._bubbleUp(this.heap.length-1); }
    else if (val > this.heap[0]) { this.heap[0] = val; this._siftDown(0); }
    return this.heap.length === this.k ? this.heap[0] : null;
  }
  // ... helpers omitted for brevity
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for deletions from the stream?
- What changes if k can grow or shrink over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioned sequences with a non-additive batch cost and compares memoization vs tabulation. It also probes edge-case reasoning when K bounds vary.

## Key Concepts
- DP over contiguous partitions
- Range max tracking
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(a, K){
  const N=a.length, INF=1e18;
  const Kcap=Math.min(K,N);
  const dp=Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=>Array(Kcap+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
    for(let k=1;k<=Math.min(i,Kcap);k++){
      let maxV=0;
      for(let j=i-1;j>=0;j--){
        maxV = Math.max(maxV, a[j]);
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + maxV);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][Kcap];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you push to O(NK) with a monotone optimization?
- How would you adapt if batch cost were sum of maxes with a penalty per batch?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses dynamic programming fundamentals through practical string segmentation, testing understanding of both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic Programming: memoization vs. tabulation
- String matching optimization (startsWith, trie)
- Time and space trade-offs between DP approaches
- State definition and transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function minWordSegmentsTopDown(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
  memo[n] = 0;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    let minSegs = Infinity;
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        const result = dfs(i + word.length);
        if (result !== -1) {
          minSegs = Math.min(minSegs, 1 + result);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[i] = minSegs === Infinity ? -1 : minSegs;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulated solution
function minWordSegmentsBottomUp(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[n] = 0;

  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        if (dp[i + word.length] !== Infinity) {
          dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], 1 + dp[i + word.length]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[0];
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time:** O(n × m × L) where n = |s|, m = dict size, L = avg word length. Using a Trie reduces this to O(n × L).
- **Space:** O(n) for the memo/DP array.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates dynamic programming skills with state expansion, requiring candidates to track whether the cell removal has been used. It tests proper handling of grid boundary conditions and provides opportunities to discuss space optimization strategies and compare memoization versus tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with auxiliary state tracking
- Space optimization using rolling arrays
- Boundary condition handling
- State transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPathWithOneRemoval(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));

  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i-1][j]);
      }
      if (j > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i][j-1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.min(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
- Time: O(m × n) - we process each cell once
- Space: O(m × n) - can be optimized to O(n) using rolling arrays since we only need the previous row
